        passage: "Akio and Lilja grow up in very different cultures, and this shapes their daily lives. Akio lives in Japan and gets up at six. He makes his bed, puts on his uniform and eats a typical Japanese breakfast with rice, miso soup and grilled fish, together with his family. After packing a lunch box, he takes the train to school. Japanese schools expect discipline and high standards. Akio likes maths and physics because they teach him to solve problems. When lessons finish, the students clean their classrooms and then go to clubs, for example judo, calligraphy or robotics. Akio belongs to the robotics club and helps build and code robots. After the clubs end, he goes to extra lessons at a cram school so he is ready for the exams to get into university. At home he does his homework and reads manga. Lilja lives in Finland and wakes up at seven. She showers, puts on casual clothes and has bread with cheese or yoghurt. She cycles to school. In Finland the lessons stress teamwork and creative thinking. At break time Lilja goes outdoors to talk with friends and get some fresh air. Not every school has clubs, but some offer music, art or sports. Lilja joins a photography club to take pictures of nature. At home she helps with chores, does an hour of homework and plays the violin. Her family often watches nature documentaries together. Akio's day is full of study and rules, whereas Lilja's has more creativity, time outdoors and family time.",
